.page xargs 1 build and run commands from standard input

# SYNOPSIS
*xargs* [*-0rt*] [*-n* _N_] [*-I* _STR_] [_COMMAND_ [_ARGUMENT_...]]

# DESCRIPTION
`xargs` reads words from standard input and runs _COMMAND_ (`echo` if none is given) with those words added to its arguments. Words are separated by white space; single quotes, double quotes and backslashes can protect white space inside a word. If there are more words than fit in one run, or `-n` limits them, the command is run several times.

# OPTIONS
@ *-n* _N_
    Use at most _N_ words per command.
@ *-I* _STR_
    Run the command once per input line, replacing _STR_ in the arguments by that whole line (spaces included). Implies `-n 1`.
@ *-0*
    Words are separated by NUL bytes, as written by `find -print0`; nothing else is special.
@ *-t*
    Print each command line on standard error before running it.
@ *-r*
    Do not run the command at all when the input is empty.

# EXIT STATUS
0 when every run succeeded, 123 when some run exited with a nonzero status, 127 when the command was not found, 126 when it could not be started.

# EXAMPLES
    ls /bin | xargs echo
    echo a b c | xargs -n1 echo
    printf 'x\ny\n' | xargs -I{} echo got-{}
    find . -name '*.tmp' -print0 | xargs -0 rm

# SEE ALSO
find(1), echo(1)
